  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}\u2061t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} & ={sec}^{2}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}\u2061t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & ={tan}^{2}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}\u2061t+1={sec}^{2}\u2061t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}\u2061t+1 & ={sec}^{2}\u2061t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
